# {{AGENT_NAME}} — Mattermost Notify Agent (operations)

AI-written notifications pushed to Mattermost, plus optional trigger-word Q&A.
Installed by [ai-automations](https://github.com/makasanaakshay/ai-automation-master);
re-run its installer to update (answers saved in `automation.config.json`).

```
ship:     release published        → GitHub-native trigger → push.sh (incoming webhook, markdown)
incident: monitored workflow fails → GitHub-native trigger → push.sh
ask:      trigger word in channel  → relay (token check, immediate courtesy reply)
                                     → repository_dispatch → push.sh (answer to the channel)
```

| Handler | Trigger | Output |
|---|---|---|
| `ship` | `release: published` | Plain-language what's-new announcement (grounded — never invented) |
| `incident` | monitored `workflow_run` fails | Calm what/impact/likely-cause note with the run link |
| `ask` | trigger word via outgoing webhook | Q-quoted, repo-grounded answer posted to the channel |

Stateless — notifications are one-shot; there's no state branch. Mattermost's
outgoing webhooks allow one immediate JSON reply, so the relay says "on it"
and the real answer lands via the incoming (notify) webhook — point both at
the same channel. Outgoing webhooks fire only in **public** channels, and
verification is a plain token compare (no HMAC — that's all Mattermost offers).

## Operating

- **Manual test:** Actions → *Mattermost Notify Agent* → *Run workflow* → mode.
- **Transcripts:** every run uploads `.agent-out/` as an artifact.
- **Model:** `AI_MODEL` in `scripts/mattermost-agent/env.sh` (currently `{{AI_MODEL}}`, brain `{{BRAIN_NAME}}`).
- **Pause ship/incident:** `gh workflow disable "Mattermost Notify Agent"`.
- **Pause ask:** delete the outgoing webhook (Integrations → Outgoing Webhooks).
- **Incident watch list:** the `workflow_run.workflows` list in `.github/workflows/mattermost-agent.yml` — edit and merge to change.
- **Agent identity:** posts show as `{{AGENT_NAME}}` only when "Enable
  integrations to override usernames" is on in the System Console — harmless
  if off (the server ignores the field).

## Local dry-run (safe — no AI, nothing sent)

```bash
DRY_RUN=1 MODE=ship RELEASE_TAG=v0.0.0 bash scripts/mattermost-agent/agent-run.sh
DRY_RUN=1 MODE=incident WF_NAME=CI bash scripts/mattermost-agent/agent-run.sh
DRY_RUN=1 MODE=ask ASK_TEXT="how does login work?" bash scripts/mattermost-agent/agent-run.sh
```

## Guardrails

- One message per event; pushes only through the configured incoming webhook.
- No invented features, no speculative root causes stated as fact; `ask` never writes code.
- `--dangerously-skip-permissions` only inside the disposable CI runner; local runs are `DRY_RUN` and stop before any AI call.
